Subject: [PATCH 0027] Give the stream window the pointer and the keyboard

The stream window stands in front of the interface, and a compositor gives the
keyboard to the front window. This Weston has no xdg-activation, so asking for
the focus back cannot work: the interface stayed inactive for as long as the
stream ran, and the stream item ignores every pointer and key event unless its
window is active. The only key that arrived was F11, because the stream window
handled that one itself, and nothing clicked in the game.

Input therefore has to be read where the compositor sends it. The stream window
takes the whole surface as its input region, listens to the pointer as well as
to the keyboard it already had, and forwards both to the host through the
streamer's input API: absolute pointer positions in the frame's own pixels,
buttons, the wheel, and keys mapped to Windows virtual keys with the same
physical table the Qt item uses. F11 keeps switching between the windowed and
the fullscreen form, and keys still held when the window goes away are released
so the game is not left walking.

Positions are scaled from the surface to the frame, so the windowed 1280x720
form reports the same coordinates as the fullscreen one, and the local cursor is
hidden because the game draws its own.

The video target links Qt Gui and nothing else, so it does not reach for the
streamer itself: it takes a small table of hooks that the render callback fills
in with the streamer's input calls. That keeps the surface free of the Rust FFI
and of Qt Quick, which it has no business including.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]

